"The internet began with a crash. On October 29, 1969, a student at UCLA tried to send the word login to a computer at a research institute about 560 kilometers away. The letters L and O arrived, and then the system crashed. So the first message on the network that grew into the internet was simply LO. In 1974, Vint Cerf and Bob Kahn described a set of rules called TCP/IP, and the network switched to those rules on January 1, 1983. The internet still runs on them today.": "b41446cb71cc",
"To cross the ocean, the packets travel through undersea cables. Almost all data that goes between continents uses these cables, not satellites. In deep water, a cable is about as thick as a garden hose, and inside are glass fibers thinner than a hair that carry the data as flashes of light. Light in glass travels about 200,000 kilometers every second, so even the 12,500 kilometers from Changhua to Boston take only a fraction of a second.": "124ecc372c29",
